// std/livecfg.jtr — a configuration that follows its file, and never half-way.
//
// `std/config` merges sources by precedence, `std/ini` reads the file above it, and
// `std/syswatch` says when a directory changed. Each of them said composing them was the
// caller's job; this is that composition, done once, so the property every service wants
// from a reloadable config is proven here rather than re-derived per service.
//
// ─── A reload that fails keeps the previous configuration WHOLE ────────────────────
//
// This is the whole design. A reload never touches the live configuration. It builds a
// CANDIDATE — a clone of the base (schema + every non-file source) with the new file
// applied on top — and only when the file validated completely does the candidate become
// the configuration, in one swap. A file with one bad value and three good ones applies
// NONE of them: the old configuration stands, every problem is recorded with its byte
// span, and `render_problems` turns them into `file:line:col` through `std/diag`.
//
// The alternative — apply key by key and stop at the first problem — leaves a service
// running a configuration nobody ever wrote, which is a bug that reproduces only under
// the exact edit that triggered it. `a_reload_that_fails_validation_keeps_the_previous_
// configuration_whole` writes a file with one good change beside one bad value and
// asserts the good change did NOT land.
//
// Building the candidate from the BASE rather than from the current configuration is
// what makes a key REMOVED from the file revert to its default: `apply` in `std/config`
// can set a value but never unset one, and a merge in place would keep every stale file
// value forever. `a_key_removed_from_the_file_reverts_to_its_default` pins it.
//
// ─── Precedence stays a property of the source ─────────────────────────────────────
//
// The candidate starts as a clone of the base, which already holds the environment and
// command-line values at their own origins, so a file value for a key the environment
// set arrives as `CFG_SHADOWED` and changes nothing. That is `std/config`'s rule applied
// unchanged: a reload is a `file` source and a `file` source does not outrank `env`.
// **A shadowed file value is therefore NOT validated** — `config.apply` answers shadowed
// before it parses — and this module treats shadowed as "not a problem", as `config`'s
// own comment says it is. A file whose only fault is a bad value for a key the
// environment overrides reloads clean; the day the override goes away, the next reload
// reports it. That is a decision a caller could disagree with, so it is stated.
//
// ─── The generation moves when the EFFECTIVE configuration does ───────────────────
//
// `generation` counts applied reloads that changed at least one value. Re-saving a file
// unchanged, or editing only its comments, answers `LIVE_SAME` and leaves the generation
// alone — so a caller that caches something derived from the configuration compares one
// integer and rebuilds only when it must. The reload's OUTCOME says whether the file was
// re-read; the generation says whether that mattered.
//
// ─── Change notification, redaction respected ──────────────────────────────────────
//
// One callback per changed KEY, with the old and new values RENDERED — through
// `config.render_value`, which redacts by declaration. A secret key reports that it
// changed, as `**** -> ****`, and never its text; the suite asserts the token's bytes are
// absent from everything the callback saw. An unset value renders as the empty string,
// which is indistinguishable from an empty string value; the report is about the key
// moving, and `origin_of` says the rest.
//
// ─── Polling, not a thread ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `step` is one pass of the caller's own loop: it drains the watcher and re-reads the
// file only when something changed. This tree's event-loop tier is single-threaded on
// purpose (`std/runtime`), and a reload that ran on another thread would hand a service a
// configuration mid-request. On Windows the watcher reports THAT the directory changed and
// never what, and on Linux it names the entry until its queue overflows — so any change
// under the directory triggers a re-read, and `LIVE_SAME` is the cheap answer when it was
// some other file. `reload_now` is the same step without the watcher, for a caller with
// its own trigger (a signal, an admin endpoint, a test that wants an exact transcript).
//
// It is called `step` and not `poll` because `poll` is `std/syspoll`'s C symbol, and an
// extern's name is taken in every module that links it — `std/syswatch` brings `syspoll`
// in, so a Jestyr `poll` here is a duplicate definition. The same rule moved `connect`,
// `close` and `listen` out of `std/httpd`.
//
// No debounce. An editor may write a file in pieces, and a step between two pieces reads
// a half-written file — which fails validation and keeps the previous configuration,
// which is exactly the property above doing its job; the next notification re-reads the
// finished file. A debounce is a policy with a latency cost, and it belongs to the caller
// that knows its editor (`syswatch_demo.jtr` shows one).
//
// ─── What it does NOT do ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// No watching of the file's PARENT for the file to appear: a watcher on a directory that
// does not exist yet is a retry policy. No hot-swap of the schema: keys are declared once,
// before the first load, because a key declared after a reload would exist in the
// configuration but not in the base the next candidate is built from. No env or CLI
// parsing: those arrive through `apply`, as `std/config` takes them.
//
// Worked example:  jestyrc run examples/std/livecfg_demo.jtr
// Run the tests:   jestyrc test examples/std/livecfg_test.jtr

import "mem"
import "list"
import "sink"
import "config"
import "ini"
import "diag"
import "syswatch"
import "runtime"
import "fs"
import "time"

// What one reload step did.
pub const LIVE_QUIET: i32 = 0        // the watcher reported nothing; the file was not read
pub const LIVE_SAME: i32 = 1         // re-read and validated; no effective value moved
pub const LIVE_CHANGED: i32 = 2      // applied; the generation advanced
pub const LIVE_REJECTED: i32 = 3     // the file did not validate; the previous configuration stands
pub const LIVE_UNREADABLE: i32 = 4   // the file could not be read; the previous configuration stands

// Room for one rendered key name, one old value and one new value when a change is
// reported. A value longer than this is cut, and the sink counts the loss — a change
// report is a notification, not the value's transport.
const LIVE_SLOT: usize = 512

// How many drain rounds `poll` allows the watcher before giving up on convergence — the
// same bound `syswatch_test` uses.
const LIVE_DRAIN_ROUNDS: usize = 16

// The change callback: `on_change(ctx, key, old, new)`. The `ctx` + fn-pointer shape
// `mem.Allocator` established — the only way a fn pointer carries state.
@copy pub struct Change {
    ctx: *mut u8,
    on_change: fn(*mut u8, str, str, str),
}

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn change(ctx: *mut u8, f: fn(*mut u8, str, str, str)) -> Change {
    return Change{ ctx: ctx, on_change: f }
}

// The callback a `Live` starts with: never called, because `has_cb` is false until
// `on_change` installs one. It exists so the field is total rather than optional.
fn ignore_change(ctx: *mut u8, key: str, old: str, new: str) { }

@no_alloc @no_os pub fn no_change() -> Change {
    return Change{ ctx: null, on_change: &ignore_change }
}

// A reloadable configuration. Owns two `config.Config`s: `base` holds the schema and every
// non-file source, `cur` is the effective configuration a reader sees. Everything the
// module touches outside itself — the clock, the filesystem, the watcher — was handed in.
@move pub struct Live {
    a: Allocator,
    clock: time.Clock,
    f: fs.Fs,
    w: syswatch.Watcher,
    path: String,
    base: config.Config,
    cur: config.Config,
    generation: i64,
    applied_at: i64,
    reloads: i64,
    rejections: i64,
    last_src: String,       // the text of the last file read, so problems render against it
    last: ini.Ini,          // the problems of the last read
    unreadable: bool,       // the last read found no file
    cb: Change,
    has_cb: bool,
    loaded: bool,
}

// Build one. `w` is the watcher over the file's DIRECTORY (`syswatch.open_dir`), or
// `syswatch.closed()` for a configuration driven by `reload_now` alone — `step` on a
// closed watcher answers `LIVE_QUIET` and never reads. Nothing is read here: declare the
// schema and apply the non-file sources first, then `reload_now`.
pub fn make(read a: Allocator, take clock: time.Clock, take f: fs.Fs, take w: syswatch.Watcher, read path: str) -> Live {
    var p: String = string_new()
    string_push(p, path)
    return Live{
        a: a,
        clock: clock,
        f: f,
        w: w,
        path: p,
        base: config.make(a),
        cur: config.make(a),
        generation: 0,
        applied_at: 0,
        reloads: 0,
        rejections: 0,
        last_src: string_new(),
        last: ini.make(a),
        unreadable: false,
        cb: no_change(),
        has_cb: false,
        loaded: false,
    }
}

fn nothing() { }

// Release both configurations, the recorded problems, and the watcher.
pub fn free(mut l: Live) {
    syswatch.close_watch(l.w) catch nothing()
    config.free(l.base)
    config.free(l.cur)
    ini.free(l.last)
    string_free(l.path)
    string_free(l.last_src)
}

// ---- the schema and the non-file sources ------------------------------------------

// Declare a key, as `config.declare`. Declared into both configurations so their ids
// agree; -1 if the name is taken or a load has already happened (see the header).
pub fn declare(mut l: Live, read name: str, k: i32, is_secret: bool) -> i32 {
    if l.loaded { return 0 - 1 }
    let id: i32 = config.declare(l.base, name, k, is_secret)
    if id < 0 { return id }
    config.declare(l.cur, name, k, is_secret)
    return id
}

// Offer a value from a NON-FILE source — `CFG_DEFAULT`, `CFG_ENV`, `CFG_CLI` — to both
// configurations, so it holds through every future reload. A `CFG_FILE` origin is refused
// as `CFG_SHADOWED`: the file is this module's source, and a hand-applied file value would
// outlive the file that no longer says it.
pub fn apply(mut l: Live, read name: str, read text: str, from: i32) -> i32 {
    if from == config.CFG_FILE { return config.CFG_SHADOWED }
    let r: i32 = config.apply(l.base, name, text, from)
    if r != config.CFG_OK { return r }
    config.apply(l.cur, name, text, from)
    return r
}

// Install the change callback. Fires once per key whose effective value moved, on the
// reload that moved it, before `generation` advances.
pub fn on_change(mut l: Live, read cb: Change) {
    l.cb = cb
    l.has_cb = true
}

// ---- reading -------------------------------------------------------------------------

pub fn generation(read l: Live) -> i64 { return l.generation }

// The clock's reading when the current generation was applied; 0 before the first.
pub fn applied_at(read l: Live) -> i64 { return l.applied_at }

// Applied reloads (changed or same) and rejected ones, so a config that never moved is
// distinguishable from one that was never read.
pub fn reloads(read l: Live) -> i64 { return l.reloads }
pub fn rejections(read l: Live) -> i64 { return l.rejections }

pub fn watching(read l: Live) -> bool { return syswatch.is_open(l.w) }

// The watcher's handle and poller, so a caller with a `runtime.Runtime` can register the
// configuration as one more thing its loop waits on (`runtime.watch(rt, handle(l), tok,
// task)` under `set_poller(rt, poller(l))`) and call `step` when the task fires — instead
// of stepping on a timer. **The pairing `std/syswatch` asks for holds here too**: the two
// come from the same watcher and mean nothing apart.
pub fn handle(read l: Live) -> i64 { return syswatch.handle(l.w) }
pub fn poller(read l: Live) -> runtime.Poller { return syswatch.poller(l.w) }

pub fn by_name(read l: Live, read name: str) -> i32 { return config.by_name(l.cur, name) }
pub fn get_int(read l: Live, id: i32) -> i64 { return config.get_int(l.cur, id) }
pub fn get_bool(read l: Live, id: i32) -> bool { return config.get_bool(l.cur, id) }
pub fn value_is(read l: Live, id: i32, read s: str) -> bool { return config.value_is(l.cur, id, s) }
pub fn origin_of(read l: Live, id: i32) -> i32 { return config.origin_of(l.cur, id) }
pub fn is_set(read l: Live, id: i32) -> bool { return config.is_set(l.cur, id) }

// `section_key` over the effective configuration, so a component holding a
// `config.Section` reads through the `Live` without reaching into it.
pub fn section_key(read l: Live, read s: config.Section, read leaf: str) -> i32 {
    return config.section_key(l.cur, s, leaf)
}

// The effective configuration, as `config.render`: name order, secrets redacted.
pub fn render(mut sk: sink.Sink, mut buf: []u8, read l: Live) {
    config.render(sk, buf, l.cur)
}

pub fn outcome_name(r: i32) -> str {
    if r == LIVE_QUIET { return "quiet" }
    if r == LIVE_SAME { return "same" }
    if r == LIVE_CHANGED { return "changed" }
    if r == LIVE_REJECTED { return "rejected" }
    if r == LIVE_UNREADABLE { return "unreadable" }
    return "?"
}

// ---- reloading -----------------------------------------------------------------------

// Is problem `i` of `x` one that invalidates the file? A shadowed value is not — see the
// header.
fn is_fault(read x: ini.Ini, i: usize) -> bool {
    if ini.problem_code(x, i) != ini.INI_REFUSED { return true }
    return ini.problem_cfg(x, i) != config.CFG_SHADOWED
}

// How many of the last read's problems invalidate the file. Zero after a clean reload,
// and zero after `LIVE_UNREADABLE` — that case is `unreadable()`.
pub fn problem_count(read l: Live) -> usize {
    var n: usize = 0
    var i: usize = 0
    let total: usize = ini.problem_count(l.last)
    for i < total {
        if is_fault(l.last, i) { n = n + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return n
}

pub fn unreadable(read l: Live) -> bool { return l.unreadable }

// Report every changed key between `cur` and `cand` through the callback. Runs BEFORE the
// swap, so the old values are still the live ones; returns how many keys moved, which is
// what decides the generation.
fn report_changes(mut l: Live, read cand: config.Config) -> i64 {
    var moved: i64 = 0
    let n: i32 = config.declared(cand) as i32
    var kraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, LIVE_SLOT)
    var oraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, LIVE_SLOT)
    var nraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, LIVE_SLOT)
    var kbuf: []u8 = slice(u8, kraw, LIVE_SLOT)
    var obuf: []u8 = slice(u8, oraw, LIVE_SLOT)
    var nbuf: []u8 = slice(u8, nraw, LIVE_SLOT)
    var id: i32 = 0
    for id < n {
        if config.same_value(l.cur, cand, id) == false {
            moved = moved + 1
            if l.has_cb {
                var ks: sink.Sink = sink.new()
                var os: sink.Sink = sink.new()
                var ns: sink.Sink = sink.new()
                config.render_name(ks, kbuf, cand, id)
                // Rendered through `config`, so redaction is the declaration's decision
                // here exactly as it is in `render`.
                config.render_value(os, obuf, l.cur, id)
                config.render_value(ns, nbuf, cand, id)
                l.cb.on_change(l.cb.ctx, sink.view(ks, kbuf), sink.view(os, obuf), sink.view(ns, nbuf))
            }
        }
        id = id + 1
    }
    free_ptr(nraw)
    free_ptr(oraw)
    free_ptr(kraw)
    return moved
}

// Read the file, validate it against the schema, and apply it whole or not at all.
//
// The steps, in order: read (an unreadable file is `LIVE_UNREADABLE` and nothing else
// happens); build the candidate from the base; parse the file into it; if any problem
// remains that is not a shadowed value, drop the candidate and answer `LIVE_REJECTED`
// with the problems kept for `render_problems`; otherwise report each moved key, swap,
// and answer `LIVE_CHANGED` or `LIVE_SAME`. The generation advances only on `CHANGED`.
pub fn reload_now(mut l: Live) -> i32 {
    l.loaded = true
    let path: str = string_view(l.path)
    if fs.present(l.f, path) == false {
        l.unreadable = true
        return LIVE_UNREADABLE
    }
    var src: String = fs.open_text(l.f, path)
    l.unreadable = false

    var cand: config.Config = config.clone(l.base, l.a)
    var x: ini.Ini = ini.make(l.a)
    ini.load(x, cand, string_view(src), config.CFG_FILE)

    // Keep this read's text and problems whatever the verdict, so `render_problems`
    // always describes the most recent file.
    ini.free(l.last)
    l.last = x
    string_free(l.last_src)
    l.last_src = src

    if problem_count(l) > 0 {
        config.free(cand)
        l.rejections = l.rejections + 1
        return LIVE_REJECTED
    }

    let moved: i64 = report_changes(l, cand)
    config.free(l.cur)
    l.cur = cand
    l.reloads = l.reloads + 1
    if moved == 0 { return LIVE_SAME }
    l.generation = l.generation + 1
    l.applied_at = time.read_nanos(l.clock)
    return LIVE_CHANGED
}

// One pass of the caller's loop: drain the watcher, and re-read the file only if it
// reported a change. Never blocks. A closed watcher answers `LIVE_QUIET` forever.
pub fn step(mut l: Live) -> i32 {
    if syswatch.is_open(l.w) == false { return LIVE_QUIET }
    var evraw: *mut syswatch.Change = alloc(syswatch.Change, 8)
    var evs: []syswatch.Change = slice(syswatch.Change, evraw, 8)
    var nraw: *mut u8 = alloc(u8, 512)
    var names: []u8 = slice(u8, nraw, 512)
    // Drain until empty: one save is several notifications on both platforms, and the
    // two coalesce differently. Bounded, as `syswatch.drain`'s header asks.
    var seen: usize = 0
    var got: usize = 1
    var rounds: usize = 0
    for got > 0 and rounds < LIVE_DRAIN_ROUNDS {
        got = syswatch.drain(l.w, evs, names) catch 0
        seen = seen + got
        rounds = rounds + 1
    }
    free_ptr(nraw)
    free_ptr(evraw)
    if seen == 0 { return LIVE_QUIET }
    return reload_now(l)
}

// ---- reporting -----------------------------------------------------------------------

// Render every problem of the last read as a `std/diag` diagnostic against the file's
// own text — `file:line:col` and a caret under the key — in the plain theme. An
// unreadable file renders one line. Writes nothing after a clean reload.
//
// The caller must check `sink.overflowed(sk)`.
pub fn render_problems(mut sk: sink.Sink, mut buf: []u8, read l: Live) {
    let path: str = string_view(l.path)
    if l.unreadable {
        sink.put_str(sk, buf, "error[E-LIVE]: cannot read `")
        sink.put_str(sk, buf, path)
        sink.put_str(sk, buf, "`; the previous configuration stands")
        sink.put_nl(sk, buf)
        return
    }
    var m: diag.SourceMap = diag.map_new(l.a)
    let fid: diag.FileId = diag.add_file(m, path, string_view(l.last_src))
    let total: usize = ini.problem_count(l.last)
    var i: usize = 0
    for i < total {
        if is_fault(l.last, i) {
            let sp: diag.Span = diag.span(fid, ini.problem_start(l.last, i), ini.problem_end(l.last, i))
            var d: diag.Diagnostic = diag.new_error(l.a, "E-INI", ini.code_name(ini.problem_code(l.last, i)))
            let cf: i32 = ini.problem_cfg(l.last, i)
            if cf >= 0 {
                diag.primary(d, sp, config.outcome_name(cf))
            } else {
                diag.primary(d, sp, "here")
            }
            diag.render(sk, buf, m, d, diag.plain())
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
}
